    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Argentine ant (Linepithema humile), native to South America, has invaded areas with Mediterranean climates worldwide, displacing many native ant species. In northern California, the Argentine ant competes for the use of scale insects as a food resource with the native winter ant (Prenolepis imparis). While previous research shows that the Argentine ant recruits nestmate foragers to a food source faster than does the winter ant, the winter ant has not been completely displaced. Earlier studies show that the winter ant's chemical defenses and differences in seasonal activity may contribute to their continued coexistence. However, the behavioral mechanisms of competition for a food source have not been determined. To investigate this, we tested the effects of priority on the outcome of recruitment to a bait to determine the winner of varying trials of food source competition. In bait trials, we varied the sequence at which ants of each species arrived at the bait and the number of ants first at the bait. The results show that the effects of the order of discovery of a food source on the outcome of competition significantly determined the victor for both species. Priority by winter ants had a stronger effect on Argentine ants than the reverse. This may help to explain the continued coexistence of the two ant species, despite the recruiting success of the invasive Argentine ant. These observations, along with other studies of changing distributions of the two species in the sympatric range, suggest that the Argentine ant will not completely displace the winter ant within northern California.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
